1
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:06,400
The third way to fight deadlocks that 
is left is to avoid the hold and wait. 

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:12,640
Simply put, we can devise a solution in which 
philosophers agree on who is going to eat  

3
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:19,120
next rather than on who gets a certain chopstick.
You have such an approach presented here: we have  

4
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:26,560
one semaphore per philosopher, an extra semaphore 
called mutex, and an array of size n of states.  

5
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:31,040
One state can either be 
HUNGRY, THINKING, or EATING. 

6
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:38,000
To enter and exit the critical section 
we have two methods: enterCS and exitCS. 

7
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:45,360
In the enterCS method, each philosopher waits on 
the shared mutex variable. In other words, only  

8
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:51,680
one philosopher at a time can run the following 
two instructions, which change his/her state to  

9
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:57,920
HUNGRY and call the help method.
After calling signal on mutex,  

10
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:04,560
the philosopher running the entryCS method starts 
waiting for a signal on its entry of the S array. 

11
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:11,360
So, how does the help function work? 
Only if the philosopher invoking it  

12
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:17,280
is hungry and his/her neighbors are not eating, 
then it changes the state of the philosopher  

13
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:24,160
invoking it to eating and sends a signal to it.
Notice that since help runs in between a wait and  

14
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:31,920
signal on the shared variable mutex, if multiple 
neighboring philosophers try to change their state  

15
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:39,760
from HUNGRY to EATING, only one manages to do so.
Once a philosopher is done eating, then he/she  

16
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:45,760
runs the exitCS method. Again, the relevant 
instructions are run inside a wait/signal  

17
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:49,840
on the shared variable mutex.
In this case, the philosopher  

18
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:56,560
sets his/her state to THINKING and then helps 
his/her neighbors, trying to send them a signal  

19
00:01:56,560 --> 00:02:01,360
they can use to enter their critical 
section (assuming they indeed want to).

20
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:07,200
This solution is valid when 
it comes to mutual exclusion,  

21
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:11,760
and it also avoids deadlocks.
nonetheless, a process can starve. 

22
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:15,200
As done before, try to reason 
formally about these properties.

23
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:18,960
If you are interested in a 
solution that also ensures  

24
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:23,440
fairness, you can find a paper 
discussing it in the attached link.

